<BaseLayout title="Veterans: Beyond the Thank You" description="Respect should have a practical side."><article class="prose"><header class="page-hero"><a href="/advocacy/">← Advocacy</a><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:18px">Starter draft · awaiting Beth’s personal edit</p><h1>Veterans: Beyond the Thank You</h1><p class="intro">Respect should have a practical side.</p></header><p>“Thank you for your service” can be a kind beginning. It should not be the end of our curiosity or care. Veterans are people with different histories, identities, needs, and plans for what comes next.</p><p>Support can be ordinary: listen without demanding a war story, offer a hand without making assumptions, and follow through when someone asks for help. Nobody owes us a heroic version of their life.</p><p>There is room here for complicated feelings about service and for lives that do not fit the recruiting poster. Respect means leaving space for the actual person.</p></article></BaseLayout>
